    <summaries>
      <summary>
        <versionCode>00</versionCode>
        <actionDate>2026-09-30</actionDate>
        <actionDesc>Introduced in Senate</actionDesc>
        <updateDate>2026-10-30T17:37:10Z</updateDate>
        <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Bipartisan American Affordability and Jobs Act of 2026</strong></p><p>This bill modifies federal permitting processes, including environmental reviews, to expedite a wide range of projects.</p><p>The bill limits the scope of the National Environmental Policy Act and expedites environmental reviews, including by&nbsp;</p><ul><li>further limiting the&nbsp;types of federal actions that trigger environmental review,</li><li>narrowing the range of alternatives that must be considered in environmental reviews, and</li><li>exempting additional types of projects from environmental review.</li></ul><p>It also establishes deadlines&nbsp;for issuing permits once an environmental review is complete.</p><p>The bill also limits permit obligations under&nbsp;the Clean Water Act, including by</p><ul><li>narrowing the scope of review for water quality certifications,</li><li>extending the term for permits issued under the National Pollutant Discharge Elimination System, and</li><li>limiting the time period for review of permits for discharges of dredged or fill materials.</li></ul><p>It also modifies requirements concerning threatened or endangered species to expedite the review of federal permits, including by shortening the timeline for certain consultations.</p><p>Additionally, the bill addresses numerous aspects of energy infrastructure permitting, including by&nbsp;</p><ul><li>expanding the authority of the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (FERC) to issue construction permits for certain interstate electricity transmission projects;&nbsp;</li><li>directing&nbsp;FERC to establish ratepayer protections related to certain large computational loads (e.g. data centers);&nbsp;</li><li>removing permit requirements for onshore oil and gas drilling on nonfederal land; and</li><li>accelerating renewable energy, geothermal, hydropower, or offshore energy projects.</li></ul><p>It also limits the scope of the National Historic Preservation Act, including by limiting what types of projects trigger review.</p>]]></text>
      </summary>
    </summaries>
